    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Unified by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> in 927, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> became one of the most centralised kingdoms of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</li><li>It developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a> and Parliament, institutions later carried across the world.</li><li>Joined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> under one crown in 1603, it merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a> in 1707.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a>, compiled in 1086 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, surveyed more than 13,000 places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and is one of the most complete records of any medieval kingdom.</p></aside>
    </section>

