    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great.jpg" alt="Alfred the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfred_the_Great,_silver_penny;_struck_875%E2%80%93880_(obverse).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG - Classical Numismatic Group, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> grew out of the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> (r. 871–899) held off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> and fortified Wessex with a network of burhs, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a> and daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed">Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians</a>, reconquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a> in the 910s. Edward’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> completed the work: in 927 he took York from its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> rulers and on 12 July received the submission of the northern rulers, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-ii-of-scotland">Constantine II of Scotland</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eamont-bridge">Eamont Bridge</a>, an event usually taken as the founding moment of a single English kingdom. He styled himself king of the English, and in 937 he defeated a coalition of Scots, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-strathclyde">Strathclyde Britons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> Vikings at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-brunanburh">Battle of Brunanburh</a>. Unity remained fragile. Viking kings, the last of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-bloodaxe">Eric Bloodaxe</a>, ruled York at intervals until 954, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready">Æthelred the Unready</a> renewed Danish attacks led to the conquest of England by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-forkbeard">Sweyn Forkbeard</a> in 1013 and by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut</a> in 1016. Cnut and his sons ruled until 1042, when the old West Saxon line returned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a>. His death in January 1066 opened a succession crisis that ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/london"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="London" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:London_Skyline_(125508655).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ilya Grigorik, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was a rich agrarian country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a>, compiled in 1086, recorded a population usually estimated at around two million, most of them villagers working open fields of wheat, barley and oats under manorial lords. The population grew to perhaps five million or more by 1300 before famine and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1348–1349, which killed perhaps a third to a half of the people, cut it drastically; labour shortages loosened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> and helped provoke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peasants-revolt">Peasants’ Revolt</a> of 1381. Wool was the great export of the medieval kingdom, shipped to the cloth towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, from 1363 largely through the staple at Calais, and from the fifteenth century England increasingly exported finished woollen cloth instead. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> grew into one of the largest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, with more than half a million people by 1700. The Church, under the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> and York, owned a large share of the land until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> dissolved the monasteries between 1536 and 1541. The universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, which emerged in the late twelfth and early thirteenth centuries, trained clergy and officials. From the late sixteenth century overseas trade expanded through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartered-company">chartered companies</a> such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, founded in 1600, and through colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> in 1607.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bannockburn"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-bannockburn.jpg" alt="Battle of Bannockburn" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bannockburn">Battle of Bannockburn</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Bannockburn.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kings relied on the fyrd, the shire levies, supported by fortified burhs and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut</a>, a paid household of housecarls. After 1066 the Norman kings ruled through castle-building and feudal knight service, and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-plantagenet">Plantagenet</a> successors fought on several fronts. Edward I conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in 1277–1283 and secured it with castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caernarfon">Caernarfon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-conwy">Conwy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harlech">Harlech</a>, but his attempt to subdue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> failed, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-ii-of-england">Edward II</a> was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bannockburn">Battle of Bannockburn</a> in 1314. In the Hundred Years’ War against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, English armies combining longbowmen with dismounted men-at-arms won famous victories at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Battle of Crécy</a> in 1346, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poitiers-1356">Battle of Poitiers</a> in 1356 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Battle of Agincourt</a> in 1415, and Henry V conquered Normandy. All was lost after the French recovery; defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-castillon">Battle of Castillon</a> in 1453 left only Calais, which fell in 1558. The dynastic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a> were effectively decided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry Tudor</a>’s victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bosworth-field">Battle of Bosworth</a> in 1485. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> built up a permanent navy, and in 1588 English ships helped defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-armada">Spanish Armada</a>. The Civil War of 1642–1651 produced the professional New Model Army, victorious at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-naseby">Battle of Naseby</a> in 1645. By the early eighteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was a leading naval and military power, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">Duke of Marlborough</a> its armies helped win the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blenheim">Battle of Blenheim</a> in 1704.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magna-carta.jpg" alt="Magna Carta" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magna_Carta_(British_Library_Cotton_MS_Augustus_II.106).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Original authors were the barons and King John of England. Uploaded by Earthsound., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was already a remarkably centralised kingdom. It was divided into shires and hundreds, run by ealdormen and royal reeves who became sheriffs; the king issued written writs in English, consulted the witan, or council of leading men, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edgar-king-of-england">Edgar</a>’s coinage reform of about 973 controlled a uniform <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coinage struck at dozens of mints. The Norman kings preserved these structures and added feudal tenure and the Exchequer, which audited sheriffs’ accounts by the early twelfth century. Henry II (r. 1154–1189) developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a>, with itinerant royal justices, standard writs and sworn juries, and in 1215 rebellious barons forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/runnymede">Runnymede</a>, establishing that the king was bound by law. Parliament grew out of the great council: Simon de Montfort’s assembly of 1265 included knights and burgesses, and by the fourteenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-commons-of-england">Commons</a> sat separately and their consent was needed for taxation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>’s break with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, enacted through Parliament with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-supremacy-1534">Act of Supremacy</a> of 1534 and directed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cromwell">Thomas Cromwell</a>, made the king head of the English Church and extended the reach of the state. Conflict between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-stuart">Stuart</a> kings and Parliament led to civil war, the execution of Charles I and a republic, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> of 1688 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bill-of-rights-1689">Bill of Rights</a> of 1689 confirmed parliamentary supremacy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/the-canterbury-tales"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-canterbury-tales.jpg" alt="The Canterbury Tales" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/the-canterbury-tales">The Canterbury Tales</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canterbury_Tales_characters_around_table_%E2%80%94_traced.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DemonDays64, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>’s institutions and culture had an influence far beyond its borders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jury-trial">trial by jury</a>, parliamentary government and the principle that rulers are subject to law, symbolised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a>, were carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, Ireland and the English colonies and later shaped the legal and political systems of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and many other countries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-language">English language</a>, transformed after 1066 by French vocabulary, became a major literary language with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-chaucer">Geoffrey Chaucer</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-canterbury-tales">Canterbury Tales</a></em> in the late fourteenth century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-caxton">William Caxton</a> printed the first books in England in the 1470s. The Elizabethan and Jacobean age produced the drama of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-shakespeare">William Shakespeare</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-marlowe">Christopher Marlowe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ben-jonson">Ben Jonson</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-james-version">King James Bible</a> of 1611 shaped English prose for centuries. In the seventeenth century England became a centre of scientific inquiry: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> was founded in 1660, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a> published his <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a></em> in 1687, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-hobbes">Thomas Hobbes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a> laid foundations of modern political thought. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and given its liturgy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cranmer">Thomas Cranmer</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a></em>, became the mother church of the worldwide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglicanism">Anglican Communion</a>. English trading companies and colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and India laid the foundations of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i.jpg" alt="Elizabeth I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darnley_stage_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> did not collapse but merged into a larger state. It had expanded within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a> over centuries: Edward I conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, which was formally incorporated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laws-in-wales-acts-1535-and-1542">Laws in Wales Acts</a> of 1535 and 1542, and English kings ruled the Lordship of Ireland from the 1170s and, from 1542, a Kingdom of Ireland. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> died childless in 1603, her cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a> became James I of England, uniting the two crowns while leaving two separate kingdoms with their own parliaments, churches and laws. The upheavals of the seventeenth century, from the Civil War and the Commonwealth of England proclaimed after the execution of Charles I in 1649 to the restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-england">Charles II</a> in 1660 and the Glorious Revolution of 1688, strained this union. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">English Act of Settlement</a> of 1701 settled the crown on the Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a>, fears that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> might choose a different successor to the childless Queen Anne, together with Scotland’s heavy losses in the failed Darien scheme, led both parliaments to negotiate a full union. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-union-1707">Acts of Union</a>, ratified by the Scottish Parliament in January 1707 and by the English Parliament in March 1707, created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a> on 1 May 1707, ending England’s existence as a separate state, although its law, church and identity survived within the new kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, unified by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> in 927 and transformed by the Norman Conquest, built one of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s most centralised governments, developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a> and Parliament, produced literature and science of world importance, and laid the foundations of the British state and empire before its union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 1707.</em></p>

